  "body": "<p>History was made in Beijing on Saturday when <a href=\"https://www.wtatennis.com/players/328120/qinwen-zheng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Zheng Qinwen</a> defeated Elise Mertens to become the first Chinese finalist in the history of the China Open, which dates back to 2004.</p>\n<p>Zheng won eight of the final 10 games against Mertens to reach her second career WTA 1000 final, building on a resurgent two months that could propel her back into the Top 30 of the PIF WTA Rankings.</p>\n<p>She'll play Mirra Andreeva in the final on Sunday. Andreeva breezed past rising star Nikola Bartunkova 6-2, 6-2, and she'll be playing for her third WTA 1000 title.</p>\n<p>Ahead of the championship match in Beijing, we make the case for both players.</p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n</div><h4>The Case for Mirra Andreeva</h4>\n<p>It's all coming up roses for Andreeva, who will be the clear favorite in this final. The fourth seed has now won 10 consecutive sets in Beijing, and she was clinical in her semifinal win over Bartunkova, dropping just four games against a player having the tournament of her career. Andreeva was steady and efficient, with 16 winners and 10 unforced errors, and continued her excellence on return, a point of emphasis for Andreeva and her team of late.</p>\n<p>The World No. 5, who qualified for the WTA Finals on Friday and is set to rise to a career-high No. 3 in the PIF WTA Rankings next week, converted five of her 10 break-point opportunities. That brings her total to 301 on the season, which leads the WTA Tour Driven by Mercedes-Benz.</p>\n<p>Despite being just 19, Andreeva also comes into this match with the edge in experience on the biggest stages. This will be her fifth title match of the season, and she's already won three of them, including her first major title at Roland Garros. </p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n        <aside class=\"instagram-post embeddable-widget embeddable-widget--floating embeddable-widget--cen