8px solid transparent; transform: translateY(-4px) translateX(8px);\"></div></div></div> <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; flex-grow: 1; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 24px;\"> <div style=\" background-color: #F4F4F4; border-radius: 4px; flex-grow: 0; height: 14px; margin-bottom: 6px; width: 224px;\"></div> <div style=\" background-color: #F4F4F4; border-radius: 4px; flex-grow: 0; height: 14px; width: 144px;\"></div></div></a><p style=\" color:#c9c8cd; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; font-size:14px; line-height:17px; margin-bottom:0; margin-top:8px; overflow:hidden; padding:8px 0 7px; text-align:center; text-overflow:ellipsis; white-space:nowrap;\"><a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DeTqYc2uYjb/?utm_source=ig_embed&amp;utm_campaign=loading\" style=\" color:#c9c8cd; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; font-size:14px; font-style:normal; font-weight:normal; line-height:17px; text-decoration:none;\" target=\"_blank\">A post shared by WTA (@wta)</a></p></div></blockquote>\n<script async src=\"//www.instagram.com/embed.js\"></script></aside>\n</div><p>Andreeva also figures to be the fresher and more well-rested player, coming off a brisk 1 hour and 14 minute semifinal and four consecutive straight-set matches. She's played five fewer sets than Zheng, whose 13 hours and 33 minutes on court are the most of any player at any event this season.</p>\n<p>On Sunday, the crowd will surely be behind Zheng when they step onto Diamond Court. Andreeva understands that, and will be ready for it. It won't be the first time she's had to manage that kind of environment.</p>\n<p>\"I have some experience playing against local players,\" Andreeva told reporters. \"I think that's going to be fine. Of course, we all know that she's playing at home. That's OK.\"</p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n<div class=\"livelike-embed widget  \">\n    <section\n        class=\"livelike-embed__content\"\n        data-widget=\"livelike/render-livelike-embed\"\n        data-widget-id=\"7764cda3-4b71-4704-b0ea-c5910a59d645\"\n        data-widget-type=\"Text Poll\"\n    ></section>\n</div>\n</div><h4>The Case for Zheng Qinwen</h4>\n<p>Even if Andreeva is unfazed by playing Zheng on her home soil, it will surely give the World No. 54 a tremendous boost in the title match, as it has all tournament.</p>\n<p>Zheng, who got into the draw as a wild card, fought her way through four consecutive three-set matches to start the tournament, two of which were upsets of Anna Kalinskaya and Marie Bouzkova. And despite the toll that those marathons surely took on her, they appear to have only made her stronger and more confident. Zheng's wins over Elina Svitolina and Mertens were in straights, and in the latter match Zheng looked the part of a former Top 5 player. She was aggressive but controlled, with 32 winners to 16 unforced errors, and her serve was a weapon, winning 73 percent of her service points (including 88 percent of first-serve points) and hitting eight aces.</p>\n<p>On her return, she only faced two break points.</p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n        <aside class=\"instagram-post embeddable-widget embeddable-widget--floating embeddable-widget--centered-mobile \"><blockquote class=\"instagram-media\" data-instgrm-captioned data-instgrm-permalink=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeUHX9xGrhQ/?utm_source=ig_embed&amp;utm_campaign=loading\" data-instgrm-version=\"14\" style=\" background:#FFF; border:0; border-radius:3px; box-shadow:0 0 1px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.5),0 1px 10px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.15); margin: 1px; max-width:540px; min-width:326px; padding:0; width:99.375%; width:-webkit-calc(100% - 2px); width:calc(100% - 2px);\"><div style=\"padding:16px;\"> <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeUHX9xGrhQ/?utm_source=ig_embed&amp;utm_campaign=loading\" style=\" background:#FFFFFF; line-height:0; padding:0 0; text-align:center; text-decoration:none; width:100%;\" target=\"_blank\"> <div style=\" display: flex; flex-direction: row; align-items: cent